%========  SUBRESP  ================
% ID TAG  IDTAG  SUBTAG 
%
% ID
% ID of the subresponse

% TAG 
% is the category of the SUBRESP. Could be related to  DISP / GENDEF / GENFORCE / STRAIN / STRESS / WEIGHT / FREQUENCY / MODE 

% IDTAG
% If the response mode is SING it is the ID specific entity to which  the DV refers. 
% If the response mode is MULT it is the ID of a SET of entities to be aggregated
% subTAG  
%  Available options depend on the TAG   ( three fields SUBTAG1, SUBTAG2, SUBTAG3 )
%
% SUBTAG1: ( if TAG = DISP ) : UX, UY, UZ, RX, RY, RZ, MAGU, MAGR 
%          ( if TAG = GENDEF    and IDTAG refers to a CPlate  ):  EPS01, EPS02, GAMMA012, K11, K22, K12, GAMMA1, GAMMA2 
%          ( if TAG = GENDEF    and IDTAG refers to a CBar    ):  EPS0, K2, K3, GAMMA2, GAMMA3, BETA
%          ( if TAG = GENFORCE  and IDTAG refers to a CPlate  ):  N11, N22, N12, M11, M22, M12, V1, V2
%          ( if TAG = GENFORCE  and IDTAG refers to a CBar    ):  N, M2, M3, T2, T3, MT
%          ( if TAG = STRESS    and IDTAG refers to a CPlate  ):  XX, YY, XY, XZ, YZ, VM
%
% SUBTAG2: (  IDTAG refers to a CPlate  ):  GP0, GP1, GP2, GP3, CENTROID, N0, N1, N2, N3 
%          (  IDTAG refers to a CBar  ):    GP0, GP1, CENTROID, N0, N1
%
% SUBTAG3: ( if TAG = STRESS  and IDTAG refers to a CPlate  ):  TOP, MID, BOTTOM 
% 
% Example
$INISUBRESP
1  STRESS     1  VM   CENTROID   TOP                      
$ENDSUBRESP

%


%========  RESP

% ID   MOD   IDSUBRESP(if MOD is MULT)  AGGREGMODE  %EXTRA1 (if AGGREG is KS) 
%
% ID
% is the ID of the response
% MOD
% Could be SING or MULT.   If is SING it refers directly to only one subresponse. IF MULT, it refers to a group of Subresponses
% IDSUBRESP
% id of the subresponse (if MOD=SING) or group (if MOD=MULT)
% AGGREGMODE  (only for MOD= MULT)
% Is the way the group of subresponses are aggregated. Could be  SUM, L1, AVG,  L2, KS , KSadv
% EXTRA1   (only for MOD= MULT)
% Are double values to calibrate the KSadv method 


 %Example
$INIRESP
1     MULT      1   KS     50.0     
$ENDRESP


%========  SET
%ID   id1   id2   id3   id4   id5  ....   +
%+  ...  idn-1    idn
%
% ID: id of the set
% id1 ... idn : ids of the entities   
%
% Can use the THROUGH keyword to define an interval of ids
$INISET
SET    1   1   THROUGH  18400   
$ENDSET